// src/user/aml/aml_fan.pdx — R31.M4-002 (#1104)
//
// The AML reader/writer for the ACPI 6.5 §11.7 cooling-device methods
// a fan-class actuator advertises -- `_STA` (present status, returns
// an integer), `_ON_` (turn the actuator on, zero-argument Method) and
// `_OFF` (turn the actuator off, zero-argument Method). This module is
// the door through which those methods reach the userspace side of the
// cooling subsystem, and eventually -- via a supervisor-side syscall
// path that does not yet exist -- KIND_COOLING_DEVICE (0x159) in the
// kernel.
//
// =====================================================================
// PLACEMENT -- CONSTITUTIONAL
// =====================================================================
// Userspace only. See design/acpi/no-aml-in-kernel.md. This file
// evaluates firmware bytecode (indirectly, through aml_eval_method) and
// must never link into src/kernel/**; tools/lint-no-kernel-aml.sh
// refuses aml* symbols there and would fail the build if a well-meaning
// refactor moved the writer into ring 0.
//
// =====================================================================
// WHY THIS IS A THIN MODULE OVER aml_eval AND NOT A SECOND EVALUATOR
// =====================================================================
// The same argument aml_thermal.pdx makes, and it is worth restating
// once for the writer side. Every ACPI 6.5 §11.7 cooling-device method
// this reader touches is a plain Method with zero declared arguments
// (`_STA` returns an integer; `_ON_` and `_OFF` have no return value
// per §5.6.4 and only cause a side effect against the platform), so
// Method invocation, integer-return handling, fuel accounting and depth
// bounding are exactly what aml_eval delivers. So this module does
// three things and no more:
//
//   (1) Resolve the child NameSeg exactly inside the CoolingDevice's
//       own scope. Exactly, not by search, for the same reason
//       aml_thermal_read_child uses aml_eval_find_in_scope rather than
//       aml_eval_resolve: a search would find some ancestor's `_OFF`
//       and drive an unrelated fan.
//   (2) Recognise the two legal shapes for `_STA` -- Method(_STA,0)
//       {Return(N)} or Name(_STA,N) -- and pick the right primitive for
//       each; recognise the Method-only shape for `_ON_` and `_OFF`.
//   (3) EXPOSE THE ACTUATION AS A NAMED ENTRY POINT (aml_fan_set_on /
//       aml_fan_set_off) rather than a raw invoke. The name is what
//       makes an audit record legible in a stack trace, and it is what
//       makes the "which fan was turned off in which round" question
//       answerable one method deep instead of after unwinding an
//       arena-index resolution.
//
// It does NOT construct a KIND_COOLING_DEVICE mint payload, and it does
// NOT drive COOLING_OP_REPORT. Those are supervisor concerns: the
// syscall wall through which a userspace writer reaches the kernel-side
// kind is R31.M4-003+ work and cannot happen from a module whose effect
// row lists only {mem}. Writing them here would make this module both
// the AML actuator and the acpi_supervisor's cooling shim, which is the
// two-things-in-one shape ec_query_pump exists NOT to be.
//
// =====================================================================
// WHY `_ON_`/`_OFF` AND NOT `_SET_STATE`
// =====================================================================
// The R31 catalog (issue #1104's summary) names the actuation
// "_SET_STATE / EC OpRegion", using _SET_STATE as a placeholder for
// "whichever ACPI method sets the cooling state". ACPI 6.5 spells that
// down in three concrete cases:
//
//   * §11.7 COOLING DEVICES: the actuator has a fixed on/off surface
//     and the methods are `_ON_`(void) and `_OFF`(void), both
//     zero-argument. THIS FILE HANDLES THIS CASE.
//
//   * §11.7 THROTTLE / P-STATE CONTROLLERS: the actuator has a range
//     0..N and the state is written to a Register OperationRegion, not
//     to an AML method at all. That path is a KIND_OP_REGION write and
//     lives in a separate module the R31.M4-003 EC-OpRegion work will
//     introduce.
//
//   * §11.7 DEVICE POWER STATES: the actuator drives `_PSx` (four
//     methods: `_PS0`, `_PS1`, `_PS2`, `_PS3`), each zero-argument. A
//     future module `aml_pstate.pdx` can follow this file's pattern
//     when it is needed; it is not needed for T14 G4 fan control.
//
// A one-argument `_SET_STATE(state)` shape appears in some vendor
// tables as sugar over `_ONx`/`_OFF`, but a one-argument Method needs
// an evaluator primitive `aml_eval_method_arg1` that does not exist and
// is deferred to R31.M4-004. Documenting the two shapes here means the
// deferral is a named absence rather than a silent one, and it means
// the follow-up issue has a specific hook (aml_fan_set_state) to fill
// in.
//
// =====================================================================
// SHAPES AND THEIR REFUSALS
// =====================================================================
// `_STA` is READ, so it accepts both shapes (see aml_thermal.pdx for
// the argument). `_ON_` and `_OFF` are ACTIONS, and §5.6.4 permits
// only the Method form -- a Name(_ON_, 1) would declare a constant and
// evaluating it as a call has no defined side effect. Anything else --
// a Method with non-zero arity, a Name for `_ON_`/`_OFF` -- is
// AML_FAN_ERR rather than a silent zero, because the whole posture of
// the AML subsystem is that a WRONG SIDE EFFECT IS WORSE THAN NONE.
//
// The arity check is the same one aml_thermal_read_child makes and for
// the same reason: a Method whose final NameSeg happens to spell `_ON_`
// is not necessarily the `_ON_` this cooling device declared, and
// invoking it with a garbage argument budget would produce a fault the
// caller would be blamed for.
//
// =====================================================================
// UNITS
// =====================================================================
// `_STA` returns bit-packed presence flags per ACPI 6.5 §6.3.7:
//
//   bit 0  set: device is present
//   bit 1  set: device is enabled and decoding its resources
//   bit 2  set: device should be shown in UI
//   bit 3  set: device is functioning properly
//   bit 4  set: battery is present (device-class specific; ignored here)
//   bits 5..: reserved, must be zero
//
// A fan whose `_STA` returns 0x0F is present, enabled, visible and
// working. THE ORDINAL IS STORED AS-IS AND NEVER INTERPRETED here;
// interpretation lives in the ring-3 supervisor. The one comment in
// this module mentioning bit meanings does so to say that this reader
// does not.
//
// =====================================================================
// THE THREE POSSIBLE OUTCOMES OF A READ, AND OF A WRITE
// =====================================================================
// The same shape aml_thermal.pdx uses, so the two modules' surfaces
// compose without a caller having to remember which returns what.
//
//   AML_FAN_OK       -- returned as a bare 0..u32max value (for reads),
//                       or as 0 (for writes: the actuator returns
//                       nothing, so "the write succeeded" is 0 and
//                       "the write faulted" is AML_FAN_ERR).
//   AML_FAN_ABSENT   -- the CoolingDevice did not declare this method.
//                       Legal for `_STA` (a fan without _STA is
//                       "present and functioning" by §6.3.7 default);
//                       an actuator refusal (the caller decides whether
//                       to treat as fatal) for `_ON_`/`_OFF`.
//   AML_FAN_ERR      -- evaluator latched. The caller must
//                       aml_eval_reset before the next attempt and
//                       should assume the actuator is unreachable this
//                       boot until proven otherwise.
//
// Both sentinels are chosen at the top of the u64 range, so they
// cannot collide with a plausible integer value: 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE as
// a _STA bit mask is unrepresentable (only bits 0..4 are ever set).
// This is the same disambiguation shape aml_thermal uses.
//
// =====================================================================
// DEPENDENCIES
// =====================================================================
// This module reads through the accessor API only -- it touches no
// arena directly and holds no state of its own. tools/verify-aml-
// parser.sh has one MODULES entry to add and no new check_confined
// call, so adding aml_fan.o strengthens every existing storage
// confinement by proving the writer reaches none of it.
//
// The four aml_eval calls this file makes -- aml_eval_find_in_scope,
// aml_eval_method, aml_eval_read_named, aml_eval_err (and the one
// aml_node_kind / aml_method_argcount pair) -- are exactly what a
// zero-argument invocation module needs. That the actuator can be
// written as ninety lines of dispatch over the existing surface is the
// evidence that #1054-#1060's evaluator was factored the right way.

module AmlFan = structure {

  // =====================================================================
  // The three §11.7 NameSegs, encoded as little-endian u32 to match
  // aml_lex_nameseg's packing. Written as the exact byte values a
  // memory dump would show.
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'S' = 0x53, 'T' = 0x54, 'A' = 0x41 -> 0x4154535F
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'O' = 0x4F, 'N' = 0x4E, '_' = 0x5F -> 0x5F4E4F5F
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'O' = 0x4F, 'F' = 0x46, 'F' = 0x46 -> 0x46464F5F
  // =====================================================================

  pub let AML_FAN_SEG_STA : u64 = 0x4154535F
  pub let AML_FAN_SEG_ON  : u64 = 0x5F4E4F5F
  pub let AML_FAN_SEG_OFF : u64 = 0x46464F5F

  // =====================================================================
  // Outcome sentinels. The top two u64 values so the failure predicate
  // is a single unsigned compare (`value >= AML_FAN_ABSENT`) rather
  // than two.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let AML_FAN_ABSENT : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE
  pub let AML_FAN_ERR    : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
  pub let AML_FAN_OK     : u64 = 0

  // The CoolingDevice / Device kind, mirrored from aml_arena.pdx's
  // node-kind enumeration. Named here rather than imported so a future
  // kind renumbering fails to compile at exactly one place per module
  // that depends on the value.
  //
  // ACPI cooling devices are declared as Device(...) in the namespace
  // (there is no dedicated CoolingDevice node kind; the presence of an
  // `_ON_`/`_OFF` pair inside a Device is what makes it a cooling
  // device per §11.7). AML_NODE_DEVICE is the arena kind for both.
  pub let AML_FAN_NODE_KIND : u64 = 6

  // =====================================================================
  // aml_fan_read_child(fan_node, seg) -> integer | AML_FAN_ABSENT | AML_FAN_ERR
  //
  // The read-side workhorse. Same shape as aml_thermal_read_child, so
  // the two modules' surfaces compose cleanly. `_STA` is the only
  // reader today; providing a generic dispatcher over any child means
  // a future `_STR`/`_PXM`/etc. reader adds a wrapper rather than
  // duplicating the two-shape logic.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_fan_read_child : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (fan_node: u64) (seg: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M4-002 (#1104): resolve NameSeg rsi as an exact child of Device node rdi and read its integer value. FOUR OUTCOMES, THREE PATHS, same shape as aml_thermal_read_child. (a) rdi == 0 or its arena kind != AML_FAN_NODE_KIND (6) -> AML_FAN_ERR, because the caller has handed us a scope with no fan semantics and returning ABSENT would let a subsequent walker treat an unrelated Method's zero as 'this cooling device is present and functioning' -- which per §6.3.7 default it in fact is when _STA is absent, but that default should be derived from ABSENT and not manufactured out of a wrong-kind scope. (b) aml_eval_find_in_scope answers 0 without latching -> AML_FAN_ABSENT. (c) A resolved AML_NODE_METHOD (4) with argcount 0 -> aml_eval_method; a resolved AML_NODE_NAME (5) (delegated to aml_eval_read_named, which enforces data-kind 1 = Integer) -> the value. Any other node kind, or a Method with non-zero arity, is AML_FAN_ERR: the resolver matched a same-named object that is not one of ACPI 6.5 §11.7's methods, and evaluating it would produce a wrong answer for the wrong reason. THE aml_eval_err POST-CHECK IS LOAD-BEARING for the same reason it is in aml_thermal_read_child: aml_eval_method's return of 0 is ambiguous between 'the method returned zero' and 'evaluation faulted'. THE aml_eval_retval_is_obj POST-CHECK IS THE COUNTERPART: a Method that returned a Buffer or Package is a table shape §11.7 does not define, and while aml_eval_method unwraps Integers to their bare value it hands back an object index (with the tag set) for anything else. Locals: r12=fan_node, r13=seg, r14=resolved node / retval scratch, r15=node kind. Labels prefixed aml_fnc_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // fan_node
        mov r13, rsi;                    // seg

        cmp r12, 0;
        je aml_fnc_err;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 6;                      // AML_FAN_NODE_KIND (Device)
        jne aml_fnc_err;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_eval_find_in_scope;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je aml_fnc_maybe_absent;
        mov r14, rax;                    // resolved node

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_node_kind;
        mov r15, rax;

        cmp r15, 4;                      // AML_NODE_METHOD
        je aml_fnc_method;
        cmp r15, 5;                      // AML_NODE_NAME
        je aml_fnc_name;

        // Any other kind is a shape §11.7 does not define.
        jmp aml_fnc_err;

      aml_fnc_method:
        // A Method that takes arguments is not this method.
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_method_argcount;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fnc_err;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_eval_method;
        mov r14, rax;                    // provisional value

        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fnc_err;

        call aml_eval_retval_is_obj;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fnc_err;

        mov rax, r14;
        jmp aml_fnc_out;

      aml_fnc_name:
        // aml_eval_read_named refuses anything other than an Integer-
        // valued NAME with AML_ERR_NOT_EVALUABLE (35).
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_eval_read_named;
        mov r14, rax;
        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fnc_err;
        mov rax, r14;
        jmp aml_fnc_out;

      aml_fnc_maybe_absent:
        // find_in_scope is silent on a genuine miss. If SOMETHING
        // latched during resolution, propagate that as ERR rather than
        // papering over it as ABSENT.
        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fnc_err;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE;     // AML_FAN_ABSENT
        jmp aml_fnc_out;

      aml_fnc_err:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // AML_FAN_ERR

      aml_fnc_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // =====================================================================
  // aml_fan_invoke_child(fan_node, seg) -> AML_FAN_OK | AML_FAN_ABSENT | AML_FAN_ERR
  //
  // The write-side workhorse. INVOKES the named method on the fan for
  // its side effect and returns whether the call landed. This is the
  // primitive `aml_fan_set_on` / `aml_fan_set_off` sit atop.
  //
  // The difference from aml_fan_read_child is TWO-FOLD and both halves
  // are load-bearing:
  //
  //   1. NAME IS REFUSED FOR AN ACTION. §5.6.4 permits only Method
  //      for a method invocation; a Name(_ON_, 1) is a namespace
  //      declaration of a constant, and evaluating it has no defined
  //      side effect. A firmware that declared such a Name would be
  //      malformed; refusing here surfaces the malformation at its
  //      origin rather than letting the caller believe the fan
  //      responded.
  //
  //   2. THE RETURN VALUE IS DISCARDED, but the RETVAL TAG must still
  //      be checked, because a `_ON_` Method that returns an object
  //      index is a shape §11.7 does not define and continuing past
  //      it would let a future consumer read that object as a bit
  //      mask.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_fan_invoke_child : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (fan_node: u64) (seg: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M4-002 (#1104): resolve NameSeg rsi as an exact child of Device node rdi and INVOKE it for its side effect. Return AML_FAN_OK on a clean call, AML_FAN_ABSENT if the method was not declared, AML_FAN_ERR on any evaluator fault. TWO DIFFERENCES FROM THE READ WORKHORSE: (a) an AML_NODE_NAME with the actuator's NameSeg is REFUSED as AML_FAN_ERR, because §5.6.4 permits only Method for a method invocation and a Name(_ON_, 1) declares a namespace constant with no defined side effect -- a caller believing the fan spun up on the strength of that would be operating under a false belief the module manufactured. (b) the return value is DISCARDED but the retval-tag is still checked, because a Method that returned an object index has done something §11.7 does not define, and letting an object index propagate as if it were a well-defined 'no return' would let a future consumer misread it. Locals: r12=fan_node, r13=seg, r14=resolved node, r15=node kind. Labels prefixed aml_fni_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // fan_node
        mov r13, rsi;                    // seg

        cmp r12, 0;
        je aml_fni_err;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 6;                      // AML_FAN_NODE_KIND (Device)
        jne aml_fni_err;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_eval_find_in_scope;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je aml_fni_maybe_absent;
        mov r14, rax;                    // resolved node

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_node_kind;
        mov r15, rax;

        // Only Method is acceptable for an actuator.
        cmp r15, 4;                      // AML_NODE_METHOD
        jne aml_fni_err;

        // A Method that takes arguments is not this method.
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_method_argcount;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fni_err;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_eval_method;
        // Discard the returned value (r14 held the node; rax holds the
        // return but we do not need it for a void actuator).

        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fni_err;

        // A retval-tag set means the Method returned a non-Integer,
        // which is a shape §11.7 does not define for `_ON_`/`_OFF`.
        call aml_eval_retval_is_obj;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fni_err;

        xor rax, rax;                    // AML_FAN_OK
        jmp aml_fni_out;

      aml_fni_maybe_absent:
        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_fni_err;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE;     // AML_FAN_ABSENT
        jmp aml_fni_out;

      aml_fni_err:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // AML_FAN_ERR

      aml_fni_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // =====================================================================
  // The three §11.7 wrappers. Each is a call to the workhorse with the
  // seg baked in -- a wrapper rather than a macro so the symbol table
  // names the operation (a caller that stack-traces through a fault
  // sees `aml_fan_set_on`, not a raw seg constant) and so the pinning
  // of "which three NameSegs this module reaches" is a property of the
  // API rather than of comment discipline.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_fan_read_sta : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (fan_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M4-002 (#1104): read the CoolingDevice's `_STA` presence bits per §6.3.7. When this returns AML_FAN_ABSENT the caller may treat the fan as 'present and functioning' per the §6.3.7 default -- but MUST know it is applying a default, not reading firmware. Labels prefixed aml_fs_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x4154535F;             // AML_FAN_SEG_STA
        call aml_fan_read_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_fan_set_on : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (fan_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M4-002 (#1104): invoke `_ON_` on the CoolingDevice's namespace, which per §11.7 SPINS THE FAN UP TO ITS ADVERTISED SPEED. AN AUDITED SIDE EFFECT ON THE MACHINE. When this returns AML_FAN_ABSENT the caller must decide whether the platform's default (`fan off`) is acceptable for the callsite; refusing rather than silently spinning up is what makes 'this fan does not have an _ON_ method' distinguishable from 'this fan is off'. When this returns AML_FAN_ERR the actuator's state is UNKNOWN and the caller must not attempt an _OFF without first calling _STA to determine what happened. Labels prefixed aml_fon_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x5F4E4F5F;             // AML_FAN_SEG_ON
        call aml_fan_invoke_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_fan_set_off : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (fan_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M4-002 (#1104): invoke `_OFF` on the CoolingDevice's namespace, which per §11.7 SPINS THE FAN DOWN. AN AUDITED SIDE EFFECT ON THE MACHINE, and the one that on a laptop-scale part matters more than _ON_: a fan pinned OFF when the CPU asks for cooling is how packages get thermally throttled to death. When this returns AML_FAN_ABSENT the caller must decide whether the platform's default (`fan on`, typically) is acceptable; a policy server that treats ABSENT as 'off happened' would leave a fan spinning that the operator asked to stop, and one that treats ABSENT as 'on failed' would leave a fan stopped that the operator asked to spin. Labels prefixed aml_fof_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x46464F5F;             // AML_FAN_SEG_OFF
        call aml_fan_invoke_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // =====================================================================
  // aml_fan_is_ok(value) -> 1 | 0
  //
  // The predicate a caller needs after every read/write: value is a
  // real outcome, not a sentinel. One unsigned compare because the
  // sentinels are the top two u64 values.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_fan_is_ok : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M4-002 (#1104): 1 iff value is not one of the two failure sentinels. A single `jae` against AML_FAN_ABSENT (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE) covers both AML_FAN_ABSENT and AML_FAN_ERR because the two sentinels are the top two u64 values -- and adding a third sentinel later needs a wider window, not a second branch. Note that AML_FAN_OK is 0, which for the WRITE side is the successful outcome; the predicate does not distinguish OK-with-value from OK-void, because both are outcomes the caller can act on. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed aml_foi_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE;     // AML_FAN_ABSENT
        cmp rdi, r10;
        jae aml_foi_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      aml_foi_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame
}
